communications  card  driver  The driver provides support for PCI
communications cards containing simple communications ports, such
as NS16550‐family serial  ports  and  standard  PC‐like  parallel
ports.   The driver is called because the interfaces to these de‐
vices aren’t nearly as well defined and standard as  they  should
be.   The driver currently supports the following cards: The dri‐
ver does not support the cards: but support  for  them  (and  for
similar  cards) should be trivial to add.  The locator is used to
identify the port (starting from 0) on  the  communications  card
that  a  subdevice is supposed to attach to.  Typically, the num‐
bering of ports is explained in a card’s hardware  documentation,
and  the  port numbers used by the driver are the same as (or one
off from, e.g. the manual uses ports numbered  starting  from  1)
those described in the documentation.  The driver appeared in The
driver was written by The current design of this driver keeps any
ports  on  these  cards  from  easily  being used as console.  Of
course, because boards with those are PCI boards, they also  suf‐
fer  from  dynamic address assignment, which also means that they
can’t easily be used as console.  Some of the cards supported  by
this  driver have jumper‐selectable port clock multipliers, which
are unsupported by this driver.  Those can be easily accommodated
with driver flags, or by using a properly scaled baud  rate  when
talking to the card.  Some of the cards supported by this driver,
e.g.  the VScom PCI‐800, have software‐selectable port clock mul‐
tipliers, which are unsupported by this driver.  Those can be ac‐
commodated using internal driver flags, or by  using  a  properly
scaled  baud  rate  when  talking to the card.  Some ports use an
driver other than the machine‐independent  driver.   Those  ports
will not be able to use ports attached to devices.
